\begin_layout Standard
Decision makers often require insight into the relative strength of the elements of evidence on which their choices hinge.
 The Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) contains innate mechanisms leading to the rigorous quantification of elements' relative strength.
 Comparative judgements (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pairwise comparisons
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) produce lists of edges for a complete graph.
 Under certain conditions,
 such graphs are also directed and acyclic,
 belonging to the class of tournament graphs.
 Intensities of Importance from comparative judgements provide weights for graphs' edges.
 Relative-entropy calculations based on Laplacians for induced subgraphs quantify the relative evidentiary strength associated with the decisional evidence ommitted from them.
 A previously published case study related to an offshore-pipeline material selection demonstrates the approach.
 The graph-entropy method provides greater strength-of-contribution contrast than sensitivity analysis from the case study.
