\begin_layout Subsection*
Transformation of comparative judments into 
\emph on
priority vectors
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
AHP 
\emph on
priority vectors
\emph default
 provide the key quantitative mechanism for evidentiary aggregation in MCDM applications.
 Mechanically,
 this is accomplished by constructiong a 
\emph on
reciprocal matrix
\emph default
 from the comparative judgements for each hierarchy model.
 A reciprocal matrix is a Hermetian matrix with unit-valued diagonals and intensity-importance values assigned to off-diagonal elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Construction of reciprocal matrices from comparative judgements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the name implies,
 a reciprocal matrix is a matrix of comparative judgements within which upper-diagonal elements are reciprocals of the lower.
 Consider again a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 comprised of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 elements,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}\supseteq\left\{ e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Then the corresponding reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
1 & a_{1,2} & \cdots & a_{1,n}\\
a_{2,1} & 1 & \cdots & a_{2,n}\\
\vdots & \ddots & 1 & \vdots\\
a_{n,1} & a_{n,2} & \cdots & 1
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{i,j}=a^{-1}_{j,i}\mbox{,}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and where values of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 are assigned according to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 as specified by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:comparative-judgement-assignment,eq:reciprocal-matrix-diagonal-identity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-matrix-B1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) illustrated reciprocal-matrix construction for criterion-to-alternative hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{B_{1}}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
1 & 3 & \nicefrac{1}{3}\\
\nicefrac{1}{3} & 1 & \nicefrac{1}{5}\\
3 & 5 & 1
\end{array}\right)\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-B1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It straightforwardly arises from applying the method above to the rows in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:criterion-alternative-comparative-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Priority-vector calculation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The AHP-application literature contains different approaches to translating a reciprocial matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 for a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 into a priority vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

.
 We summarize here comparisons previously described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1984"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is a projection onto the unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta^{n}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta^{n}=\left\{ \boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\left|\sum_{i}x_{i}=1\;\wedge x_{i}\geq0\:\forall i\right.\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

is highly significant.
 All AHP calculation results are constrained to the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,\,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This inlcudes both AHP-calculation results and standardization of inputs demonstrated in a subsequent subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Summary of priority-vector approaches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We summarize here comparisons previously described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1984"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 We also make passing observation of a more-empirical approach to obtaining priority vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
Principal-eigenvector method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a,Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 strongly argued for the principal eigenvector as the definitive approach to calculating priority vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the eigenvector/eigenvalue decomposition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

of the reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 for a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

.
 As usual,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 denotes a matrix whose dimensions correspond exactly to those of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

,
 and whose columns are the eigenvectors thereof.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix of the corresponding eigenvalues and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 a vector of the diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 .
 The principal eigenvalue then appears as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}\mbox{.}\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-principal-eigenvector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The priority vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 follows from the principal eigenvector as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i}=\frac{\left[\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i,\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{i}}\left[\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i,\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}}\mbox{.}\label{eq:perron-priority-vector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 grounds the rationale for this priority-vector choice in the 
\emph on
Perron-Frobenius Theorem
\emph default
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Horn2012,Hawkins2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Priority-ordering stability by AHP proves highly sensitive to the transitive consistency of comparative judgements.
 More-strenuous than von Neumann-Morgenstern transitivity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:vonNeumann-Morgernstern-transitivity-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 AHP transitivity requires
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{i,j}a_{j,k}=a_{i,k}\mbox{.}\label{eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Transitive consistency applies not just to preferential ordering,
 but also of intensities of importance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By induction,
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) extends to higher orders
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{1,2}a_{2,3}\cdots a_{n-1,n}=a_{1,n}\mbox{.}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In fact,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 proves a limiting case
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\boldsymbol{A}^{k}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{T}\boldsymbol{A}^{k}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}=\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\label{eq:limit-perron-eigenvalue-definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

given some arbitrary positive-definite 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
 This associates 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 with the limiting case of transitive consistency specified by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
Geometric-mean methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Forman1996"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 argued that arithmetic or geometric means provided suitable priority vectors in some cases.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zeng2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 employed the geometric-mean approach
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i}=\frac{\left({\displaystyle \prod^{n}_{j=1}a_{i,j}}\right)^{\frac{1}{n}}}{{\displaystyle \sum^{n}_{i=1}}\left({\displaystyle \prod^{n}_{j=1}a_{i,j}}\right)^{\frac{1}{n}}}\mbox{.}\label{eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrated the accompanying risk of rank-reversal with this approach even under near-trivial conditions.
 Nonetheless,
 when a high degree of comparative-judgement transitivity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) occurs,
 results from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) converge to those produced by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:perron-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
 The approach (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) offers transparency and reproducibility advantages.
\end_layout

